<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute cystitis in pregnancy (O23.12)</content>. Lower urinary tract symptoms with a positive dipstick and no fever, flank pain, or tenderness over the kidneys: acute cystitis, not pyelonephritis. No signs of preterm labor. In pregnancy every urinary infection is treated and a culture is sent, because untreated bacteriuria can lead to pyelonephritis and preterm birth. [S]<list><item>Urine culture sent before treatment.</item><item>Nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily with food for 5 days; suitable at 24 weeks, and to be avoided near term.</item><item>Drink at least 2 liters a day, more when travelling.</item><item>Urine culture as a test of cure one to two weeks after treatment.</item><item>Return the same day for fever, chills, back or flank pain, vomiting, contractions, leaking fluid, or bleeding.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Fourteenth pregnancy at 37 (O09.529)</content>. Fetal heart 146 per minute; fundal height 24 cm, consistent with dates. Blood pressure 108/66 mmHg; urine protein negative. [S]<list><item>Continue aspirin and folic acid.</item><item>Prenatal visit at 27 weeks as planned, with the glucose challenge test.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Pregnancy at Age 35 Years or Older: ACOG Obstetric Care Consensus No. 11 (2022); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Asymptomatic Bacteriuria: 2019 Update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (2019)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Prenatal visit in three weeks with the culture result and a test-of-cure culture; same day for the warning signs above. Next scheduled visit 1631-04-14: prenatal visit at 27 weeks: glucose challenge, blood count, and Tdap. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
